Z||000001||rprevo||08-18-2000||07:42 AM|||| Have you tried your local Auto store?<BR>I was suprised at all the stuff I could get<BR>for a fiero at Murrays discount Auto<BR>in Pontiac Mi.<BR>I also did bussiness with kick hill once.<BR>Ordered seats and a radio, He sent the<BR>seats through the US Mail I got the first<BR>seat bottom one day the next day I didn't<BR>get nothing then I got the seat back, any<BR>way it took about 1 week and a half to<BR>get all the stuff to assemble the pair of<BR>used seats I bought. Then to top it off they<BR>were both pasenger side seats so I had to<BR>skin my old driver seat plus I never recieved<BR>the radio!! But in his defence he did send<BR>me 4 inner fenders for free over the mess.<P><BR>Ray<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000002||theogre||08-18-2000||01:39 PM||||far as I know all the gas tanks are interchangable. try your local scrap yards.<P>I've been told it's better to just call kickhill farm.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BN Boomer||08-18-2000||03:52 PM||||Ogre, I thought it's been said that the two different sizes of fuel tanks were not interchangeable. Something about the earlier cars' tunnel not being quite wide enough to accept the larger fuel tanks for 87-88s. Maybe a little research is in order here to see if the tank from my 87 parts car will work in my 85 GT. I'd sure appreciate the extra 2 gallons of capacity.||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||08-18-2000||07:30 PM||||Ya I have one. It's out of an 84 with a good fuel pump. It's been a life-long Arizona car, so it's perfect with the rust situation. However, wherever you get one, I would suggest spending the 20 bucks for the fuel pump, as it's a pain in the ass to change it later, not to mention being stranded somewhere.<P>Two problems.<P>First, shipping would be a fortune.<P>Second, the good ole post office would probably not take kindly to me shipping a potential bomb!||||reg||11
